>
  <p>A grid of sixteen endless encoders invites one predictable mistake: mapping everything that moves. The result looks productive for an hour and becomes impossible to remember a week later.</p>
  <p>A durable mapping is smaller. It gives each bank a job, reserves controls for orientation and records enough context that the Live Set can explain itself without the controller attached.</p>

  <h2>Begin with four decisions, not sixteen parameters</h2>
  <p>Write down the actions you repeat while listening. “Reduce room level on the selected stem” is a decision. “Control send B” is only an implementation. The first survives a routing change; the second becomes wrong as soon as the Set is rearranged.</p>
  <p>Group controls by moment in the workflow:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Capture:</strong> input level, monitor balance, click and cue.</li>
    <li><strong>Shape:</strong> filter, dynamics threshold, saturation and tone.</li>
    <li><strong>Space:</strong> early reflections, decay, pre-delay and send levels.</li>
    <li><strong>Performance:</strong> macros that create audible transitions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>If one bank needs a printed legend to be usable, reduce it before adding a second bank.</p>

  <h2>Map to racks when the device chain may change</h2>
  <p>Direct mappings to individual plug-in parameters are quick but fragile. An Instrument or Audio Effect Rack can expose a stable set of macros while devices underneath change. Name the macros by sound or action — “Bow edge”, “Room length”, “Pulse density” — rather than the plug-in control they currently drive.</p>
  <p>Set safe ranges. A macro that spends half its travel in unusable feedback or silence is not expressive control; it is an avoidable failure state.</p>

  <h2>Use pushes for states, turns for ranges</h2>
  <p>Where the controller exposes a push action, assign it to an actual state change: device on/off, reset, alternate bank, mute or a defined performance switch. Avoid hiding destructive actions such as record deletion or global reset behind an unlabelled push.</p>
  <p>For encoders, decide how jumps will behave when a Set opens or a bank changes. Endless encoders can reduce value jumps, but only if the MIDI mode and Live mapping behave as expected. Test every bank change at extreme parameter values.</p>

  <h2>A minimal sixteen-control layout</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Row</th><th>Role</th><th>Design rule</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>1</td><td>Primary tone</td><td>Four controls that remain useful on nearly every sound</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>Dynamics and movement</td><td>Slow-to-fast or subtle-to-obvious from left to right</td></tr>
      <tr><td>3</td><td>Space</td><td>Keep wet amount near the related time/colour controls</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>Utility</td><td>Monitor, reference, bypass and one spare — not four more effects</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Test the mapping by breaking it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Open the Set with the controller disconnected. The project must still be understandable.</li>
    <li>Connect after Live has opened. Confirm that feedback and values recover.</li>
    <li>Change banks with parameters at minimum and maximum.</li>
    <li>Duplicate or reorder tracks and see whether the mapping still targets what its label promises.</li>
    <li>Record automation, play it back, then touch the control. Confirm the takeover behavior.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Document the mapping in the Project</h2>
  <p>Save a one-page map beside the Live Set and include the controller configuration file if one is required. Record the Live version, MIDI ports and remote-control settings. A photograph of the controller is not enough; it cannot show bank logic, ranges or push assignments.</p>
  <p>The Twister may be a good fit for a particular workflow, but that conclusion needs an actual hardware test. Until then, the honest deliverable is a mapping method that can be evaluated on any suitable encoder controller.</p>
